#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125778 :tutorial/content "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A,$ and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A,$ we need to find the solution of the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}$.\n\nIn other words,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$  is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c75fc81-c464-5eea-bb66-37258ca64cb5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3520 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125779 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e46fe003-9e24-5794-b139-5c2805d463c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3521 :tutorial/title "The Nullity of a Matrix"}]}
